## 11. Deviation handling

- Any result that does not meet its predefined criterion is a Fail and is recorded as a deviation (`DEV-{{tool_id}}-<nn>`) in the deviation log of {{doc_id_05}}.
- Classification: [fill in, per the deviation procedure]. A failed gate or guardrail test, or a deviation that affects a gate, a guardrail or a record used by the validation (for example the provenance label that selects the OQ and PQ samples), is at least major and blocks release of the affected surface or context unless accepted open per {{doc_id_07}} section 7.
- Resolution options: correct and re-execute the whole test (a new run with new evidence; earlier runs are kept), or release with restrictions that exclude the affected surface or context. A re-test is valid only after a correction that addresses the failure mode; re-executing on a new sample alone does not close a deviation.
- A deviation from the protocol steps (not from the result) is documented with an impact assessment approved before continuing.
- If a deviation shows that the tool already caused a problem in use, start [fill in: CAPA / nonconformity process] and assess the impact on product and records.

